<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Sample SpiceGrinder model: the Collaborate channel worked example.

  Watcher combines its own <Input> (Source, a Constant) with two CollaborationBeacon
  instances — BeaconA and BeaconB — referenced purely via collaborate.primary/
  collaborate.secondary attributes, never as <Input> children. Neither beacon appears
  anywhere in Watcher's <Input> tree: they're absent from ModelValidator/
  ModelComplexityAnalyzer's structural checks by construction, resolved and called only
  through ICollaborationTarget.collaborate().

  Pull Watcher's output repeatedly and inspect it: the Source-derived first value changes
  each call (a normal <Input> pull), but the two beacon-derived values stay constant across
  every call — collaborate() never advances a beacon's own counter, only its own
  getNextObservation() does (which nothing in this model ever calls). That invariant is the
  entire point of this demo.

  Output: [Source value, BeaconA current value, BeaconB current value] per observation.
-->
<dataset>
	<root node="Watcher"/>
	<nodes>
		<CollaborationWatcher name="Watcher" collaborate.primary="BeaconA" collaborate.secondary="BeaconB">
			<input name="Source"/>
		</CollaborationWatcher>
		<Constant name="Source" value="1" type="double"/>

		<CollaborationBeacon name="BeaconA" initial="100"/>
		<CollaborationBeacon name="BeaconB" initial="500"/>
	</nodes>
</dataset>
